{
  "schema_version": "formalization-faithfulness-1",
  "role": "adjudicator",
  "task_id": "LEV-CH01-EIGENVALUES-WAVE-SPEEDS-CANONICAL-20260908",
  "trigger": [
    "round-trip judge requested adjudication",
    "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item N06 unclear"
  ],
  "resolved_items": [
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge requested adjudication",
      "resolution": "The target is a single-mode solution construction, whereas the selected source claim concerns propagation of the components of a general hyperbolic-system solution. The matching translation formula and differential operator do not make these assertions equivalent. Classification is not-faithful-different, independently of the unresolved source regularity convention.",
      "primary_evidence": "Attached raw PDF page 25, printed page 3, first paragraph connects a complete independent eigenvector set and unique expansion to corresponding component-wave speeds. The elaborated target instead supplies profile, speed, and one vector, assumes Ar = speed*r and a profile derivative, and concludes PDE satisfaction for the field fixed by D001 and D004."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item N06 unclear",
      "resolution": "Separate the definite completeness omission from the unspecified analytic scope. N06 fails because no complete eigenbasis, expansion, or uniqueness of expansion is represented. The source's exact regularity class remains unspecified; classical pointwise differentiation is a coherent interpretation, but equivalence with every intended source solution class cannot be established. This uncertainty does not affect the structural rejection, which persists for smooth solutions.",
      "primary_evidence": "Printed page 3 explicitly states unique decomposition of every vector in the chosen independent eigenvector set. D003 quantifies only two derivative vectors for an already specified field. D018 uses a full-neighborhood derivative assertion. Printed pages 1–3 do not specify a function space or weak-solution convention."
    },
    {
      "item": "Disputed effects of D001–D005 and reused derivative semantics",
      "resolution": "D001 and D004 define q(x,t) = profile(x-speed*t)r. D002 merely abbreviates D003. D003 requires actual derivatives of the temporal and spatial slices, together with their exact PDE residual equality; it contains no hidden decomposition or propagation characterization. D005 supplies continuous coordinatewise scalar multiplication, not an additional hypothesis on the solution. The reused interpretations are supported by the supplied declarations, but their correctness does not establish target faithfulness.",
      "primary_evidence": "The complete inline dependency inventory gives D003 as Exists qt, Exists qx, HasDerivAt (fun τ => q x τ) qt t ∧ HasDerivAt (fun ξ => q ξ t) qx x ∧ qt + coefficient.mulVec qx = 0. D005's explicit type uses the real scalar action and product topology. D001, D002, and D004 have the displayed function bodies in both dossiers."
    },
    {
      "item": "N02 classification disagreement and component identity",
      "resolution": "Ordinary vector-coordinate semantics pass: the matrix acts by row dot products, vector operations are pointwise, and derivatives use the finite product topology. Characteristic-component coverage fails: none of these definitions supplies coefficients relative to a complete eigenvector basis. Thus the round-trip failure identifies an actual omission, while the direct pass establishes only the correctness of the ambient coordinate operations.",
      "primary_evidence": "D022 defines Matrix.mulVec by row dot products; D064–D068 and D084 provide pointwise vector operations and product topology. Printed page 3 discusses coefficients in an eigenvector expansion. The target has neither a basis nor a map extracting those coefficients."
    },
    {
      "item": "Nonvacuity, zero vectors, and exceptional speeds",
      "resolution": "The theorem is nonvacuous: m=1, A=[2], r=(1), speed=2, profile(s)=s, and profile'=1 satisfy its premises and yield a nonconstant wave. Allowing r=0 adds trivial cases in which speed need not be an eigenvalue; it does not make the entire theorem vacuous or assert that every real number is an eigenvalue. Dimension zero is likewise a harmless extra degenerate case. Signed, zero, and repeated eigenvalues are not incorrectly excluded.",
      "primary_evidence": "The elaborated target requires only Ar = speed*r and HasDerivAt profile profile' (x-speed*t), without r ≠ 0, positive dimension, or restrictions on speed. D001 makes the field zero when r=0. Printed page 2 assigns speed -c to the leftward acoustic wave, and printed page 3 imposes no distinctness or nonvanishing requirement on eigenvalues."
    },
    {
      "item": "Genuine generalization versus faithful strength",
      "resolution": "The matrix-domain extension is genuine for the construction lemma: a defective matrix can support an individual traveling eigenmode. However, the target simultaneously omits the source's assertion about general solution components. It therefore cannot be accepted as faithful-stronger. A=[[1,1],[0,1]], r=(1,0), speed=1, and a smooth nonconstant profile give a substantive target instance outside the source's complete-eigenbasis setting.",
      "primary_evidence": "The target's only spectral premise is Ar = speed*r. For the displayed matrix that relation holds, but its eigenspace is one-dimensional, so it has no complete eigenbasis in R². Printed page 3 requires m independent eigenvectors for the hyperbolic system under discussion."
    }
  ],
  "implications": {
    "lean_implies_source": {
      "verdict": "no",
      "reasoning": "As a statement-faithfulness comparison, verifying the PDE for prescribed fields f(x-λt)r does not establish that the eigenbasis coefficients of an arbitrary solution propagate at their corresponding eigenvalues. Even after supplying the source's complete eigenbasis, the target provides no extraction of those coefficients or evolution characterization. Additional mathematical results are needed; this is not a definitional reformulation. The omission persists entirely within smooth classical solutions."
    },
    "source_implies_lean": {
      "verdict": "no",
      "reasoning": "The selected source claim has a complete real eigenbasis as inherited context and describes component propagation. The target universally asserts a sufficient construction under only one vector relation, including nonzero modes of defective matrices outside that context. These additional construction cases follow from calculus and matrix linearity, but are not specializations or definitional consequences of the selected source assertion. This scope comparison does not claim that the valid construction lemma is mathematically false."
    }
  },
  "classification": "not-faithful-different",
  "accepted": false,
  "remaining_uncertainties": [
    "The selected source passage does not determine an exact solution regularity class or settle the extent of intended weak-solution coverage. The target explicitly uses classical pointwise derivatives. This analytic uncertainty does not change either structural scope comparison."
  ],
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "category": "constructed-mode-versus-general-component-propagation",
      "evidence": "Printed page 3 relates component-wave propagation to complete eigenvector decomposition. D001 fixes a traveling field in advance, and D002–D003 conclude only that this field satisfies the PDE at the supplied point.",
      "impact": "The target represents a supporting construction lemma rather than the selected general component-propagation assertion."
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "category": "missing-completeness-and-changed-domain",
      "evidence": "The source requires m independent eigenvectors and states unique expansion. The target contains only Ar = speed*r and also applies to defective matrices with individual real eigenvectors.",
      "impact": "The omitted component structure and extended matrix domain prevent classification as either equivalent or faithfully stronger."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "category": "zero-vector-spectral-interpretation",
      "evidence": "The vector binder has no nonzero condition; r=0 satisfies Ar = speed*r for arbitrary speed and makes the constructed field identically zero.",
      "impact": "The parameter speed is not certified as an eigenvalue in every target instance, although substantive nonzero instances remain."
    },
    {
      "severity": "note",
      "category": "unspecified-source-regularity",
      "evidence": "Printed pages 1–3 do not specify a function space. D018 and the target's hprofile require a classical derivative at x-speed*t.",
      "impact": "Full analytic-scope equivalence remains unestablished; the definite structural mismatch already determines rejection."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The attached primary-source images and inline declaration evidence support the decision without majority voting or reliance on declaration names. The PDE, signed translation, real arithmetic, derivative witnesses, and finite-dimensional topology are correctly represented. The decisive difference is that translation is prescribed in a constructed single mode instead of derived for components of an arbitrary hyperbolic-system solution. N06's definite completeness failure is distinguished from the remaining regularity uncertainty. The theorem has nontrivial instances, but its broader matrix applicability does not restore the missing source conclusion. Source-byte and rendering hashes are supplied provenance and were not independently recomputed."
}
